exports[`fscale init --packs core,group-project > builds a repo where the pack reports no violations, with the template and guide pinned > CONTRIBUTING.md 1`] = `
"# Contributing

<!-- Created by firescale (group-project/contributing). It is your team's to edit. -->

How we work on this repo together. Read it before your first pull request.

## Set up

Describe how to install the project here.

## Check your work

Before you push, run the same check that CI runs:

\`\`\`sh
npx firescale verify
\`\`\`

It runs the firescale rules, then lint, typecheck and tests. Fix what it reports before you ask for a review. When it says \`SETUP NEEDED\`, your machine is missing something: run the command it shows, and do not change code for it.

## Branches

Do not commit to the main branch directly. Make a branch for each change, with a name that matches one of these patterns (\`*\` is any text):

- \`feat/*\`
- \`fix/*\`
- \`docs/*\`
- \`test/*\`
- \`refactor/*\`
- \`chore/*\`
- \`ci/*\`
- \`build/*\`
- \`perf/*\`
- \`style/*\`
- \`release/*\`
- \`hotfix/*\`
- \`revert/*\`

The long-lived branches are \`main\`, \`master\`, \`develop\`.

For example: \`git switch -c feat/login-page\`.

## Pull requests

- Keep each pull request to one change, small enough to review in one sitting.
- Fill in the pull request template.
- CI runs \`npx firescale verify\`. It must pass before the pull request is merged.
- A teammate reviews and approves it before it is merged.
"
`;

